window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t":"From data to inference","u":"module-1.html#big-picture","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Statistics is the art and science of learning from data. It includes designing studies and analyzing data objectively, as well as calculating numbers. Four phases connect a research question to a defensible conclusion: research question → design → description → inference . Identify subjects, the target population, the sample frame, and the sample. Distinguish categorical and quantitative variables, parameters and statistics. Describe a distribution’s shape, center, spread, and unusual observations. Calculate proportions, means, medians, sample SD, quartiles, and outlier fences. Subjects are the units we observe. The target population is everyone we want to understand. The sample frame is the list from which we select. The sample is the subset for which we obtain data. A parameter summarizes the population; a statistic summarizes the sample. Probability uses a known population to reason about samples; statistical inference uses a sample to learn about an unknown population. Worked example A new diet plan A doctor samples 30 dieting obese women following a plan and 30 not following it from her clinic list. Mean losses after six weeks are 18 and 12 pounds. The subjects are the women. The target population is all dieting obese women. The sample frame is the clinic list; the sample is the 60 selected women. The two sample means, 18 and 12, are statistics. Population means are unknown parameters. The clinic list may not represent the entire target population. A large sample cannot correct an unrepresentative frame."},{"t":"Variables and displays","u":"module-1.html#variables","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"A variable is a characteristic recorded for each subject. Categorical values identify groups: nominal categories have no natural order (cookie brand); ordinal categories do (low, medium, high). Quantitative values have meaningful numerical operations: discrete values are countable; continuous measurements can take values throughout an interval. Whole-year recorded age is discrete at that recording precision; underlying elapsed age is continuous. For categorical data, begin with a frequency table, then a bar chart (separate bars) or pie chart. For quantitative data, use a histogram (adjacent numerical bins), stem-and-leaf plot, or boxplot. Describe the number of modes, symmetry or skewness, center, spread, and outliers. A right-skewed distribution has a longer right tail; a left-skewed one has a longer left tail. “Skew” names the tail, not where most observations lie. p= Xn, percentage=100 p\\% Worked example Shark attacks The four state counts are California 187, Florida 828, Hawaii 230, Texas 58; total 1303. Choose the category of interest: Florida, with X = 828. Divide by the total: p̂ = 828/1303 = 0.6355, about 63.5%. Distinguish the categorical variable “state of an attack” from the quantitative variable “number of attacks in a state.” The way the data are recorded determines the variable. TI-84 828 ÷ 1303 ENTER Excel =828/1303 . Format as Percentage if desired. Watch out. Numbers are not always quantitative. A zip code or a jersey number is a label, so it is categorical. And a variable like \"age group: 18–24, 25–34\" is ordinal, even though it is built from numbers."},{"t":"Center: mean and median","u":"module-1.html#center","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The mean uses every observation; the median is the midpoint of the ordered data; the mode is the most frequent value. For odd n, take the middle ordered value for the median. For even n, average the two middle values. The population mean is μ; the sample mean is x̄. x= i=1n xin Worked example One outlier changes the mean For 1, 2, 3, 4, 5: x̄ = 15/5 = 3 and median = 3. Replace 5 with 100: the sum is 110, so x̄ = 110/5 = 22. The ordered middle observation remains 3. The median is resistant; the mean is pulled toward the outlier. TI-84 STAT → EDIT → enter values in L1. STAT → CALC → 1-Var Stats L1. Read x̄ and Med. Excel =AVERAGE(1,2,3,4,100) gives 22; =MEDIAN(1,2,3,4,100) gives 3. For a roughly symmetric, unimodal distribution, mean and median are close. In right-skewed data the mean is usually larger; in left-skewed data it is usually smaller. This is a useful guide, not a universal theorem for every possible data set. Common mistakes. Forgetting to sort before finding the median. With an even n , picking one of the two middle values instead of averaging them."},{"t":"Spread: work out s by hand","u":"module-1.html#spread","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The range is maximum minus minimum. Sample variance measures squared deviations from the sample mean; sample SD is its square root, in the original units. All values equal gives s = 0. Greater spread usually produces larger s. s2= (xi- x)2n-1, s= s2 Worked example SD for 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 Mean: x̄ = (1+2+3+4+5)/5 = 3. Deviations: −2, −1, 0, 1, 2. Square and add: 4+1+0+1+4 = 10. Divide by n−1 = 4: s² = 10/4 = 2.5. Take the square root: s = √2.5 = 1.5811. TI-84 1-Var Stats L1: Sx = 1.5811 . σx uses n and is a population SD. Excel =STDEV.S(A1:A5) ; variance: =VAR.S(A1:A5) . Red-pencil reminder. Divide by n−1 for a sample. Do not report variance when asked for SD. Neither the mean nor SD is resistant to outliers. Watch out. The TI shows both Sx and σx . In this course you want Sx , which divides by n-1 ."},{"t":"Quartiles, IQR, and outliers","u":"module-1.html#quartiles","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Order the observations. Find the overall median. Divide the data into a lower half and an upper half, excluding the overall median from both halves when n is odd . Q1 is the median of the lower half; Q3 is the median of the upper half. This is our TI-84 convention. IQR=Q3-Q1, fences=Q1-1.5IQR,\\;Q3+1.5IQR Worked example The five-number summary 1, 2, 7, 11, 12, 18, 23, 25, 98, 197 Median = (12+18)/2 = 15. Lower half: 1, 2, 7, 11, 12, giving Q1 = 7. Upper half: 18, 23, 25, 98, 197, giving Q3 = 25. Five-number summary: (1, 7, 15, 25, 197). IQR = 25−7 = 18. Mild fences: 7−1.5(18) = −20 and 25+1.5(18) = 52. Extreme upper fence: 25+3(18) = 79. Both 98 and 197 are extreme outliers. Boxplot whiskers reach the most extreme non-outliers: 1 and 25. Plot the outliers separately. TI-84 1-Var Stats → minX, Q1, Med, Q3, maxX. 2nd Y= → modified boxplot. Excel Sort A1:A10. =MEDIAN(A1:A5) and =MEDIAN(A6:A10) . Excel QUARTILE.INC/EXC may use a different convention. About outliers. Never delete an outlier just because it is extreme. Fix it if it is a typo; otherwise keep it in every calculation. If you report results without it, say so and say why."},{"t":"Put the description together","u":"module-1.html#summary","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Describe shape, center, spread, and outliers in context, including units. For skewed data use median and IQR; for reasonably symmetric data mean and SD are useful. Investigate an outlier before changing or removing it. Correct a recording mistake if verified; never remove a legitimate extreme value simply because it is inconvenient. Based on Dr. Saah’s Module 1 lecture notes. The SD arithmetic is expanded for exam preparation."},{"t":"Ask how two variables relate","u":"module-2.html#objectives","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Bivariate analysis asks whether values of one variable occur more often with particular values of another. An explanatory variable x predicts or defines comparison groups; a response y is the outcome. The word “explanatory” alone does not establish a causal relationship. Choose displays and summaries appropriate to both variable types. Interpret correlation, a regression line, residuals, and R². Calculate a regression line from summary statistics. Recognize extrapolation, influential points, and lurking variables. Explanatory / response Display Compare Categorical / quantitative Side-by-side boxplots Center and spread Categorical / categorical Two-way table, stacked bars Row proportions Quantitative / quantitative Scatterplot Direction, form, strength, outliers For the pet-and-stress example, the pet group’s mean heart rate is 73.48, versus 91.33 with a friend and 82.52 alone. The pet group has the lowest mean and median. Compare groups on a shared scale so graphical differences remain meaningful. More worked examples Smoking (yes/no) and survival after 20 years (yes/no): smoking is explanatory, survival is the response; both categorical. Fruit type (A, B, C) and sucrose level in grams: fruit type is explanatory and categorical; sucrose is the response and quantitative. Gasoline used daily and air pollution in ppm: gasoline is explanatory; pollution is the response; both quantitative. Worked example Pets and stress Researchers randomly assigned 45 dog owners to do a stressful task alone, with a good friend, or with their dog, and measured heart rate. Group Mean Median SD Pet 73.48 69.81 9.97 Friend 91.33 90.58 8.34 Alone 82.52 84.63 9.24 The pet group has the lowest mean and median heart rate, so the group a person was in is associated with heart rate. If all three groups had means near 74, there would be no association."},{"t":"Row proportions reveal association","u":"module-2.html#categorical","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Put the explanatory variable on rows. Within each row, divide each cell by the row total. Differences in these conditional proportions indicate association. Raw counts can mislead when groups have very different sizes. row proportion= cell countrow total Worked example Pesticides in foods Food type Pesticide None Total Conventional 19485 7086 26571 Organic 29 98 127 Conventional pesticide proportion = 19485/26571 ≈ 0.7333. Organic pesticide proportion = 29/127 ≈ 0.2283. Compare proportions, not 19485 versus 29. Conventional foods are more likely to have pesticide present in this sample. TI-84 Enter each cell count divided by its own row total. Excel =19485/26571 and =29/127 . Watch the denominator. Dividing 19485 by the grand total answers a joint question, not “among conventional foods.” Dividing by the pesticide column total reverses the condition. Worked example Academic year and employment Year Job No job Total First year 10 90 100 Sophomore 20 80 100 Junior 15 35 50 Senior 8 12 20 First year: 10/100 = 0.10 have a job. Sophomore: 20/100 = 0.20 . Junior: 15/50 = 0.30 . Senior: 8/20 = 0.40 . The proportion with a job rises every year, so academic year and employment are associated."},{"t":"Scatterplots and correlation","u":"module-2.html#correlation","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Describe a scatterplot’s direction, form, strength, and unusual observations before calculating. R summarizes direction and strength of a linear relationship. Its sign is the direction; its magnitude shows how tightly points follow a line. R is unitless, between −1 and 1, and unchanged by switching x and y or by changing measurement units through positive linear rescaling. R= 1n-1 i=1n ( xi- xsx ) ( yi- ysy ) R = 0 means no linear association, but a strong curved relationship may still exist. R = ±1 means all points lie on a line. Outliers can greatly change R. The vehicle example has R ≈ −0.866: a strong negative linear association between weight and MPG. TI-84 Enter x in L1 and y in L2. Turn DiagnosticOn on in 2nd 0 (CATALOG), then run LinReg(a+bx). Excel =CORREL(A2:A26,B2:B26) . Plot the paired values first. Always look at the scatterplot. In the car data, adding one low outlier pulled R from -0.87 to -0.45 , and a different outlier pushed it to -0.91 . A curved pattern can also give an R near 0."},{"t":"Least squares, by hand","u":"module-2.html#regression","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"A regression line predicts the response at a given explanatory value. The least-squares line minimizes the sum of squared vertical residuals . A residual is observed minus predicted. Positive residuals lie above the line; negative ones lie below. With an intercept, the least-squares line passes through (x̄, ȳ) and its residuals sum to zero. y=b0+b1x, b1=R sysx, b0= y-b1 x, e=y- y Hand method · summary-statistic illustration From summaries to an equation Suppose x̄=10, ȳ=30, sx=2, sy=6, R=0.8. Slope: b₁=0.8(6/2)=2.4 response units per x unit. Intercept: b₀=30−2.4(10)=6. Line: ŷ=6+2.4x. At x=12, prediction is 34.8. If y=32, the residual is 32−34.8=−2.8, below the line. TI-84 Use arithmetic for summary inputs. For raw paired data: STAT → CALC → LinReg(a+bx) L1,L2; a is the intercept and b the slope. Excel =0.8*6/2 ; =30-2.4*10 . With raw pairs use =SLOPE(y_range,x_range) and =INTERCEPT(y_range,x_range) . Worked example Vehicle weight and MPG Using the rounded lecture line ŷ=45.645−0.005x, a 3970-pound car gets 21 MPG. Prediction: 45.645−0.005(3970)=25.795 MPG. Residual: 21−25.795=−4.795 MPG. Slope: a one-pound increase in weight corresponds to a predicted decrease of 0.005 MPG. The intercept is not physically useful: a zero-pound car is outside the data. TI-84 45.645−.005(3970) 25.795 21−Ans −4.795 Excel =45.645-0.005*3970 ; then =21-25.795 . Full-precision fitted coefficients can give a different prediction from this rounded line."},{"t":"Fit, prediction, and influence","u":"module-2.html#fit","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Interpolation predicts within the observed x range. Extrapolation predicts outside it and is risky: the observed relationship may not continue. The infant example’s line, ŷ=−932.404+70.310x, is used for gestational ages 23–35 weeks. At 30 weeks, predicted birth weight is 1176.896 grams. Inspect the residual plot. A satisfactory straight-line fit gives an unstructured horizontal band about zero. A curve suggests a missing nonlinear pattern; fanning suggests changing variability. A large R² does not repair these problems. R2=(R)2=proportion of variation in y explained by regression on x For the vehicle example, R²≈0.751: about 75.1% of variation in MPG is explained by the linear regression on weight. It is not the percentage of points exactly on the line or a probability that the model is true. An influential point substantially changes the fitted line. An extreme x value can have high influence even if its residual is not initially large. Compare a fit with and without the point; investigate whether it is a recording error, but do not automatically delete it. TI-84 Read r² from LinReg output; STAT PLOT can plot L1 versus RESID. Excel =RSQ(y_range,x_range) . Calculate residuals as observed y minus predicted y, then scatterplot them against x. Common mistakes. Computing y - y instead of y - y , which flips the sign of the residual. Swapping sx and sy in the slope formula. Worked example Two models for a depression index (DPI) Work satisfaction gives R2 = 0.709 ; social interaction gives R2 = 0.844 . Interpretation: 70.9% of the variability in DPI is explained by the linear regression on work satisfaction. The social index model is better because its R2 is higher. A model using both variables might do better still."},{"t":"Association and causation","u":"module-2.html#causation","g":"Module 2 notes","x":"Correlation does not imply causation. A lurking variable is not included in the analysis but affects its interpretation. Town population can increase both the number of churches and the number of bars (common response). Temperature can increase both ice cream sales and swimming, creating a relationship with drowning deaths. Confounding makes the separate effects difficult to distinguish. Choose a model using subject knowledge, the scatterplot, residual behavior, and explanatory power together. Write predictions and residuals with units, interpret slopes in context, and treat extrapolation cautiously. Based on Module 2 lecture notes. The correlation formula includes the sample normalization factor explicitly."},{"t":"Good inference needs good design","u":"module-3.html#objectives","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"A poorly designed study can produce precise-looking but misleading results. Before calculating anything, ask who was studied, how subjects were selected, whether a treatment was imposed, and what population the results can reasonably describe. Separate anecdotal evidence, observational studies, and experiments. Recognize sampling designs and major sources of bias. Identify factors, levels, treatments, and experimental units. Choose a randomized design and assess control, blinding, and replication. An observational study records explanatory and response variables without imposing treatments. It may be retrospective (look backward), cross-sectional (a present snapshot), or prospective (follow forward). An experiment imposes treatments and measures outcomes. A single friend’s experience is anecdotal evidence, not an adequate substitute for a well-designed study. Worked example Two cell-phone studies A questionnaire compares past phone use in 469 patients with brain cancer and 422 without: observational, because exposure was not assigned. A study exposes 100 mice to microwaves and leaves 100 unexposed: experimental, because exposure is imposed. Animal results may not generalize to humans. An ethically unacceptable human experiment does not become acceptable just because it would answer an important question."},{"t":"How were subjects sampled?","u":"module-3.html#sampling","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"A simple random sample (SRS) gives every possible sample of size n the same chance. Equal individual selection chances are necessary but alone do not guarantee an SRS. Random sampling supports generalization to the represented population. Design What you do Key distinction SRS Randomly select from a frame All samples equally likely Systematic Random start, every kth entry Beware periodic frame patterns Stratified Sample within every stratum Some from every group Cluster Sample whole clusters All from selected groups Strata are groups whose members are similar with respect to a relevant characteristic, such as academic year. Clusters are often geographic or organizational units, ideally each reflecting population diversity. Stratification aims for representation; cluster sampling often saves travel and cost. systematic position j=r+(j-1)k Hand method · systematic sampling illustration Every tenth name For interval k=10, randomly select a start from 1 through 10. Suppose r=4. Selected positions are 4, 14, 24, 34, … The sixth selected position is 4+(6−1)(10)=54. TI-84 MATH → PRB → randInt(1,10). Then add k repeatedly. Excel =RANDBETWEEN(1,10) for the start. Freeze that value; position j is =start+(j-1)*10 ."},{"t":"Bias is not random variation","u":"module-3.html#bias","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"A sampling method is biased when it systematically favors some population members or responses. Undercoverage excludes people from the frame. Nonresponse bias occurs when selected people do not respond and differ in relevant ways from respondents. Response bias comes from inaccurate answers, social desirability, poor wording, or leading questions. Convenience samples select easily available subjects. Voluntary response samples let subjects opt in. Neither reliably represents the target population. Increasing n reduces random sampling variability but does not remove systematic bias. Worked example A lot of replies can still mislead Shere Hite’s survey was mailed to 100,000 women but received 4500 replies. Its size does not remove undercoverage or nonresponse concerns. A named paper survey can also elicit more socially desirable reports of study hours than an anonymous computer survey. Ask who had no chance to be sampled: undercoverage. Ask who was selected but did not reply: nonresponse. Ask whether recorded replies are accurate: response bias. Red-pencil reminder. “Random sampling” and “random assignment” answer different questions. Sampling helps represent a population; assignment helps create comparable treatment groups. More worked examples Classic examples Literary Digest, 1936: polled its own subscribers by phone and predicted Landon would beat Roosevelt. Roosevelt won in a landslide. Convenience sampling with severe undercoverage: in 1936, subscribers with phones were wealthier than voters overall. \"Would you have children again?\" An advice column asked readers to write in; 70% of nearly 10,000 said no. Voluntary response: people with strong negative feelings were most motivated to reply. Shere Hite's survey: mailed to 100,000 women, only 4,500 replied. Nonresponse bias: the 4.5% who answered may differ sharply from those who did not. Paper versus laptop surveys: students randomly assigned to a signed paper survey reported more study hours than those answering anonymously on a laptop. Response bias: people give socially desirable answers when they can be identified."},{"t":"Design an experiment","u":"module-3.html#experiments","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"A factor is an imposed explanatory variable. Its values are levels . A treatment is a combination of levels. An experimental unit is the smallest unit receiving a treatment independently. number of treatments= number of levels per factor Worked example Soybeans, tillage, and pesticides Two tillage types and three pesticide schedules give 2×3=6 treatments: A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3. Ten fields each have six equal plots. Randomly assign the six treatments within each field. The experimental units are the 60 plots. Field location is a blocking variable, not a treatment: it already exists and is not imposed. TI-84 2 × 3 gives six combinations. Random numbers can assign their order within each block. Excel =2*3 . For each field, attach RAND() values to the six treatments, sort, then freeze the assignment. Completely randomized: assign units directly to treatment groups. Randomized block: form similar groups first, then randomize within each group. Matched pairs: match very similar subjects into pairs and randomize within pairs. Crossover: each subject receives both treatments in random order, with appropriate attention to carryover and washout. In the running-shoe website example, putting comfort messages in the morning and discounts in the afternoon confounds treatment with time of day. Repeated use across ten days does not fix the lack of random assignment. Watch for confounding. An online shoe store shows a comfort page in the mornings and a discount page in the afternoons, then compares sales. Time of day is confounded with the page shown, because the treatment was not randomized."},{"t":"Four experimental essentials","u":"module-3.html#essentials","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"Control: a comparison treatment, standard care, or placebo helps isolate the treatment effect. A placebo can address improvement from expectation alone. Randomization: chance assignment balances known and unknown lurking variables in expectation. A single randomization does not guarantee perfectly identical groups. Blinding: subjects do not know their assignment in a single-blind study; researchers in contact with subjects also do not know in a double-blind study. Replication: apply each treatment to many experimental units to reduce random variation. This differs from reproducing an entire study. Worked example Zyban and smoking cessation The 429 smokers are randomly assigned to Zyban or nothing. Randomization and replication are present, and the untreated group is a control. Blinding is limited because there is no placebo or alternative pill. Generalization is limited by the motivated, healthy volunteer group."},{"t":"Scope and ethics","u":"module-3.html#summary","g":"Module 3 notes","x":"A well-conducted randomized comparative experiment supports causation in its setting. A representative random sample supports generalization. Neither result is automatic: nonresponse, noncompliance, measurement problems, and different populations can limit a study. Protect subjects through informed consent, confidentiality, and safeguards against harm. Evaluate what the study actually establishes before trusting a headline. Based on Module 3 lecture notes. Definitions distinguish an SRS from equal individual inclusion chances."},{"t":"A language for chance","u":"module-4.html#objectives","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"A random phenomenon has unpredictable individual outcomes but regular long-run behavior. Its sample space S contains all possible outcomes. An event is a subset of those outcomes. For equally likely outcomes, probability is favorable outcomes divided by total outcomes; do not use this counting rule if outcomes are not equally likely. Translate “and,” “or,” “not,” and “given” into event operations. Use tables and trees for joint, conditional, and reversed probabilities. Distinguish independence from disjointness and handle sampling without replacement. Find discrete means and SDs, and continuous uniform areas. The Law of Large Numbers says repeated relative frequencies tend toward the theoretical probability. It does not promise steady improvement at every trial or guarantee the next outcome will compensate for earlier outcomes."},{"t":"The probability rules","u":"module-4.html#rules","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"A union A∪B means A or B or both. An intersection A∩B means both. The complement Aᶜ means not A. Disjoint (mutually exclusive) events cannot occur together. 0 P(A) 1, P(S)=1, P(Ac)=1-P(A) P(A B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A B) If events are disjoint, the intersection is zero. Otherwise subtract the overlap so it is counted once. Worked example Diabetes treatment and complications Treatment Complications None Total 1 11 77 88 2 9 103 112 Total 20 180 200 Let A = treatment 1 and B = complications. P(A)=88/200=.44; P(B)=20/200=.10. P(A∩B)=11/200=.055. P(A∪B)=.44+.10−.055=.485. P(Bᶜ)=1−.10=.90, also 180/200. TI-84 88÷200+20÷200−11÷200 . Use parentheses when combining fractions. Excel =88/200+20/200-11/200 ."},{"t":"“Given” changes the denominator","u":"module-4.html#conditional","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"Conditioning restricts the sample space to an event already known to have occurred. The order matters: P(B|A) generally differs from P(A|B). P(B A)= P(A B)P(A), P(A B)=P(A)P(B A) Worked example A positive screening test Of 5282 screened pregnancies, 48 affected and 1307 unaffected pregnancies test positive. Six affected and 3921 unaffected test negative. We are given a positive test, so the denominator is all positives: 48+1307=1355. P(affected | positive)=48/1355=.0354, about 3.54%. Reversing the condition gives P(positive | affected)=48/54=.8889. This is a different probability. TI-84 48÷1355 , then compare 48÷54 . Excel =48/(48+1307) ; reversed: =48/(48+6) . Watch the denominator. A false positive is a positive result when the condition is absent. “Probability of a positive given no condition” differs from “probability of no condition given a positive.” Always name the condition after the vertical bar. Common mistakes. Forgetting to subtract P(A B) in the addition rule. Using the wrong denominator in a conditional probability: in P(B A) , the denominator is the \"given\" group A ."},{"t":"Independence and repeated trials","u":"module-4.html#independence","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"A B P(B A)=P(B) P(A B)=P(A)P(B) Independent events do not change one another’s probabilities. Disjoint positive-probability events are dependent: knowing one occurred rules out the other. Worked example Three unusual subjects Five of 100 people are unusual. Draw three without replacement. First unusual: 5/100. Given that success, second unusual: 4/99. Given both successes, third unusual: 3/98. P(all three)=(5/100)(4/99)(3/98)=.0000618429. TI-84 (5/100)(4/99)(3/98) . Excel =(5/100)*(4/99)*(3/98) . For independent trials with success chance p, “at least one” is easiest through its complement: P(at least one success)=1-(1-p)n TI-84 For p=.2, n=4: 1−(1−.2)^4 gives .5904. Excel =1-(1-0.2)^4 ."},{"t":"Multiply branches, then add paths","u":"module-4.html#trees","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"On a probability tree, probabilities leaving each node sum to 1. Multiply probabilities along a path for its joint probability. Add disjoint paths that lead to the desired outcome. To reverse a condition, divide a joint probability by the total of all paths into the new condition. Worked example Skin cancer by location Location probabilities are head .15, trunk .41, limbs .44. The respective probabilities of woman given location are .56, .37, .80. P(head and woman)=.15(.56)=.084. P(woman)=.15(.56)+.41(.37)+.44(.80)=.5877. For a reversed condition, P(head | woman)=.084/.5877≈.1429. TI-84 Store the branch products, add for P(woman), then divide .084 by that sum. Excel =0.15*0.56+0.41*0.37+0.44*0.8 ; reverse with =0.084/0.5877 ."},{"t":"Discrete and continuous models","u":"module-4.html#random-variables","g":"Module 4 notes","x":"A discrete random variable X has countable outcomes; add their probabilities for an event. Its probability model must have nonnegative probabilities summing to one. Its mean is a weighted average, not an unweighted mean of listed outcomes. X= xp(x), X2= (x- X)2p(x), X= X2 Worked example Impaired ears in Dalmatians P(X=0)=.70, P(X=1)=.22, P(X=2)=.08. μX=0(.70)+1(.22)+2(.08)=.38 ears. σX²=(0−.38)²(.70)+(1−.38)²(.22)+(2−.38)²(.08)=.3956. σX=√.3956=.6290 ears. TI-84 Outcomes in L1, probabilities in L2 → 1-Var Stats L1,L2. Use σx, not Sx, for the probability model. Excel =SUMPRODUCT(outcomes,probabilities) . Compute weighted squared deviations and take SQRT. A continuous random variable assigns probabilities to intervals as areas under a density curve. Total area is one. P(X=c)=0 at any single point, so endpoint inclusion does not change an interval probability. For a uniform model on [a,b], height is 1/(b−a). P(c<X<d)= d-cb-a (a c<d b) Worked example Uniform from 0 to 5 Height=1/5=.2. P(1≤X≤3)=(3−1)(.2)=.4. P(X<1 or X>3)=1−.4=.6. TI-84 (3−1)÷(5−0) . Excel =(3-1)/(5-0) . When combining independent random variables, their variances add for sums or differences. If independence fails, covariance must also be considered. Based on Module 4 lecture notes. The reversed skin-cancer calculation extends the lecture tree. Common mistakes. Forgetting to multiply by pi when finding the mean or variance. Reporting the variance when the standard deviation was asked. Treating X > 2 as if it included 2."},{"t":"Recognize a normal model","u":"module-5.html#normal-model","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"A normal distribution is symmetric, unimodal, and bell-shaped. We write X N( , ) , where the second parameter is the standard deviation , not the variance. The curve has total area one. Use a histogram, boxplot, and normal Q-Q plot to assess whether a normal model is reasonable. Q-Q points should roughly follow a straight line; strong curves, gaps, or outliers raise concerns. Apply the Empirical Rule and standardize values. Use a left-area table for normal probabilities and percentiles. Check BINS, calculate binomial probabilities, and use a normal approximation when appropriate. 68\\%: , 95\\%: 2 , 99.7\\%: 3 Worked example Exam scores: μ=80, σ=5 Within one SD: (75,85), about 68%. Within two SDs: (70,90), about 95%. Within three SDs: (65,95), about 99.7%. P(X>75)=.5+.68/2=.84 using the Empirical Rule. P(70<X<85)=.95/2+.68/2=.815. TI-84 The Empirical Rule is approximate. 2nd VARS → normalcdf(75,1E99,80,5) gives the exact model area, about .8413. Excel =1-NORM.DIST(75,80,5,TRUE) . A small difference from .84 is expected."},{"t":"Standardize before the table","u":"module-5.html#z-scores","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"z= x- , Z N(0,1) A z-score tells how many SDs an observation lies above (positive) or below (negative) its mean. Standardizing preserves the corresponding area. Draw the curve, mark the original cutoff, calculate z, then place that z under the cutoff. Worked example Adult female heights Heights are approximately N(65,3.5). Find P(X<68). z=(68−65)/3.5=.857142… Round z to .86 for the table. Find row .8, column .06. The left area is .8051. Software uses unrounded z and gives .8043. Both answers represent the same method at different precision. TI-84 2nd VARS → normalcdf(−1E99,68,65,3.5). Excel =NORM.DIST(68,65,3.5,TRUE) . TRUE requests cumulative left area. Worked example Heights Women's heights are N(65, 3.5) and men's are N(70, 4) in inches. A 72-inch woman: z = (72-65)/3.5 = 2 , two standard deviations above average. A 62-inch man: z = (62-70)/4 = -2 , two standard deviations below average. A 67-inch man: z = (67-70)/4 = -0.75 . The farther z is from 0, the more unusual the value."},{"t":"Four normal-area patterns","u":"module-5.html#normal-areas","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"Let Φ(z) denote the left area from the normal table. Round z to two decimal places and use four-decimal entries. Beyond ±3.49, the table area is essentially zero or one. P(Z>a)=1- (a), P(a<Z<b)= (b)- (a) P(Z<a or Z>b)= (a)+1- (b) Worked example Dragonfly wingspans: N(4,.25) Less than 4.3: z=(4.3−4)/.25=1.20; area=.8849. More than 4.3: 1−.8849=.1151. Between 3.6 and 4.7: z₁=−1.60, z₂=2.80; .9974−.0548=.9426. Outside 3.8 and 4.2: z=±.80; .2119+(1−.7881)=.4238. Software gives .4237. TI-84 normalcdf(lower,upper,4,.25). For outside, use 1−normalcdf(3.8,4.2,4,.25). Excel Between: =NORM.DIST(4.7,4,0.25,TRUE)-NORM.DIST(3.6,4,0.25,TRUE) . Outside: 1 minus the between area. Draw first. A “greater than” question usually needs a complement. A “between” question needs subtraction of two left areas. Do not read one table entry and stop. Worked example Women shorter than 68 inches P(X < 68) with X N(65, 3.5) . z = (68-65)/3.5 = 0.857 0.86 . Row 0.8, column .06: P(Z<0.86) = 0.8051 . Software gives 0.8043. The small difference comes from rounding z ; either is accepted in practice here. Common mistakes. Reporting the table value when the question asks for \"greater than.\" Subtracting in the wrong order for \"between\" (the answer can't be negative). Forgetting to standardize and looking up x itself in the table."},{"t":"Work backward: inverse normal","u":"module-5.html#inverse","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"When an area is given, first express it as an area to the left . Search the inside of the table, recover z from its row and column, then return to the original units. If an area falls exactly halfway between two entries, use the midpoint of the corresponding z values. x= +z Worked example A 95th percentile, and the middle 90% For dragonfly N(4,.25), the 95th percentile has left area .95. .9500 is halfway between .9495 at z=1.64 and .9505 at z=1.65. Use z=1.645. x=4+1.645(.25)=4.41125, about 4.411 inches. The middle 90% leaves 5% in each tail. Use ±1.645, giving (3.589,4.411). TI-84 invNorm(.95,4,.25); lower endpoint invNorm(.05,4,.25). Excel =NORM.INV(0.95,4,0.25) and =NORM.INV(0.05,4,0.25) ."},{"t":"Is it binomial? Check BINS","u":"module-5.html#binomial","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"B: binary outcome on each trial. I: independent trials. N: a fixed number n of trials. S: the same success probability p on every trial. “Success” simply names the category being counted. Write X Binomial(n,p) . Sampling without replacement from a small population violates independence and constant p. Our course rule of thumb is population size at least 20 times sample size. Do not confuse population N with sample n. P(X=k)= nkpk(1-p)n-k, nk= n!k!(n-k)! Worked example A weighted coin A coin has P(heads)=.75. Toss it independently four times. BINS holds: two outcomes, independent tosses, fixed n=4, fixed p=.75. For exactly three heads, C(4,3)=4!/(3!1!)=4. P(X=3)=4(.75)³(.25)=.421875≈.4219. TI-84 2nd VARS → binompdf(4,.75,3). To calculate C(4,3), enter 4 MATH → PRB → nCr → 3. Excel =COMBIN(4,3)*0.75^3*0.25 or =BINOM.DIST(3,4,0.75,FALSE) . Translate words carefully: “at most k” is X≤k; “fewer than k” is X≤k−1; “at least k” is 1−P(X≤k−1); “more than k” is 1−P(X≤k). Add exact-probability terms or use the complement to save work. Worked example At most one, and at least three For Binomial(10,.5), P(X≤1)=C(10,0)(.5)¹⁰+C(10,1)(.5)¹⁰=.0009766+.0097656=.0107. For Binomial(30,.11), P(X<3)=P(0)+P(1)+P(2)≈.3442. P(X≥3)=1−P(X≤2)≈.6558. TI-84 binomcdf(10,.5,1); 1−binomcdf(30,.11,2). Excel =BINOM.DIST(1,10,0.5,TRUE) ; =1-BINOM.DIST(2,30,0.11,TRUE) . Small populations break independence. Choosing 6 of your 20 friends, 4 of them left-handed: the chance of a lefty is 4/20 on the first pick but 3/19 or 4/19 on the next. Rule of thumb: the sample should be less than 5% of the population (population at least 20 times the sample). Worked example Malnutrition, n = 30 , p = 0.11 P(X=0) = (0.89)30 = 0.0303 . P(X=1) = 30(0.11)(0.89)29 = 0.1124 . P(X=2) = 302(0.11)2(0.89)28 = 435(0.0121)(0.0383) = 0.2015 . Fewer than 3: 0.0303 + 0.1124 + 0.2015 = 0.3442 . At least 3: 1 - 0.3442 = 0.6558 . Keep four decimals (or more) in each term and round only at the end. Your calculator's MATH PRB 3:nCr computes nk : type 30 nCr 2 ."},{"t":"Binomial center, spread, approximation","u":"module-5.html#approximation","g":"Module 5 notes","x":"=np, = np(1-p), X N\\! (np, np(1-p) ) Use the normal approximation only when np≥10 and n(1−p)≥10 . Our course uses no continuity correction : standardize the stated count directly. If the conditions fail, use the exact binomial model. Worked example A very unusual count For n=262 and p=.422, np=110.564 and n(1−p)=151.436, so both conditions pass. μ=110.564 and σ=√[262(.422)(.578)]=7.994… For at least 207, z=(207−110.564)/7.994…≈12.06. This is beyond the table: the right-tail area is essentially zero (less than .0002 by the table). TI-84 normalcdf(207,1E99,110.564,√(262*.422*.578)). Excel =1-NORM.DIST(207,110.564,SQRT(262*0.422*0.578),TRUE) . Extremely small tails may display as zero. Based on Module 5 lecture notes. Practice accepts table and full-precision software normal areas."},{"t":"Three distinct distributions","u":"module-6.html#three-distributions","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"This module connects probability to inference. A statistic changes from sample to sample even when the population and sample size stay the same. A sampling distribution describes that repeated-sampling variation. Population distribution: individual values in the population, summarized by parameters. Data distribution: individual values in one observed sample, summarized by statistics. Sampling distribution: the values of a statistic across all possible repeated samples of a fixed size. Our goals are to find the center, spread, and shape of sampling distributions; check normal-approximation conditions; and calculate probabilities and middle ranges for p̂ and x̄. Increasing n changes the sampling distribution, not the original population’s shape. p= Xn, x= xin"},{"t":"Sampling distribution of p̂","u":"module-6.html#proportions","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"A sample count X and a sample proportion p̂ describe the same successes on different scales. If n=20, possible proportions are 0, .05, .10, …, 1. The mean of repeated sample proportions is p. Their SD gets smaller as n increases. p=p, p= p(1-p)n For random, approximately independent observations, p̂ is approximately normal when np≥10 and n(1−p)≥10 . If those conditions fail, calculate probabilities through the binomial count X instead. For sampling without replacement, our course uses the population-at-least-20n rule of thumb to justify approximate independence. Worked example Why n=20 is not quite enough The claimed preference proportion is p=.57. For n=20, μp̂=.57 and σp̂=√[.57(.43)/20]=.1107. np=11.4, but n(1−p)=8.6. One condition fails. Solve both: n≥10/.57=17.54… and n≥10/.43=23.26… Use the larger bound, then round up: n≥24. At n=30, σp̂=.0904… and the normal approximation is appropriate. TI-84 Calculate √(.57*.43/30). Calculate both 10/.57 and 10/.43, then round the larger up. Excel =SQRT(0.57*0.43/30) ; =ROUNDUP(MAX(10/0.57,10/0.43),0) . Worked example A pizzeria's claim A Boston pizzeria claims 57% of Bostonians prefer its pizza. In one random sample of 20, 13 (65%) do. With n = 20 , p can be 0, 0.05, 0.10, …, 1. A thousand different samples of 20 would give many different p values. Simulating that, the 1000 values of p averaged 0.57 with a standard deviation of 0.11. With samples of 50, they still averaged 0.57, but the standard deviation shrank to 0.07."},{"t":"Probabilities and middle ranges for p̂","u":"module-6.html#proportion-probability","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"z= p-p p(1-p)/n Worked example Owls in saguaro cacti p=.40 and n=96. Find P(p̂<.27). Check np=38.4 and n(1−p)=57.6: both pass. Center=.40; SD=√[.40(.60)/96]=.05. z=(.27−.40)/.05=−2.60. Left-area table: P(p̂<.27)=Φ(−2.60)=.0047. The middle 80% uses z*=1.282: .40±1.282(.05)=(.3359,.4641). Rounding z* to 1.28 gives (.336,.464). TI-84 normalcdf(−1E99,.27,.4,.05). Middle range: invNorm(.1,.4,.05) and invNorm(.9,.4,.05). Excel =NORM.DIST(0.27,0.4,0.05,TRUE) . Endpoints: =NORM.INV(0.1,0.4,0.05) and the .9 quantile. For the pizzeria at n=30, standardize .50 and .60 with the unrounded SD. Rounded z values are −.77 and .33. The table probability is .6293−.2206=.4087, while software gives .4107. That difference is due to z rounding. Red-pencil reminder. The denominator is the SD of p̂, √[p(1−p)/n]. Do not divide by p, 1−p, or the SD of the success count. Worked example Probability for p With p = 0.57 and n = 30 , what is P(0.50 < p < 0.60) ? p = 0.57 and p = 0.57(0.43)/30 = 0.0904 . (Conditions: 30(0.57)=17.1 and 30(0.43) = 12.9 , both at least 10.) z1 = (0.50-0.57)/0.0904 = -0.77 and z2 = (0.60 - 0.57)/0.0904 = 0.33 . P = 0.6293 - 0.2206 = 0.4087 . Software gives 0.4107; the difference is rounding z . Key step. Standardize with p , the standard deviation of the sampling distribution . Dividing by p(1-p) without the n , or by p , gives the wrong answer."},{"t":"Sample means and the CLT","u":"module-6.html#means-clt","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"x= , x= n For random, independent observations, these center and spread formulas hold regardless of population shape when the population variance is finite. Shape requires an additional argument: If the population is normal, x̄ is exactly normal for any n . If the population is not normal, the CLT gives an approximately normal x̄ for sufficiently large n. Our course guideline is n≥30 ; strong skew and extreme outliers can require more. If the population is not normal and n<30, normality of x̄ is not established. For example, weekly beer counts can be very right skewed. Averaging many observations makes the distribution of means less skewed and narrower. It does not transform individual beer counts into normal data. Worked example When we can't use the normal model The number of people students dated in the past month has = 0.854 , = 1.024 , and is extremely right-skewed. With n = 10 , the population isn't normal and n < 30 , so the sampling distribution of x is unknown. We cannot find P( x > 2) with the normal table."},{"t":"Use the spread of the mean","u":"module-6.html#mean-probabilities","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"z= x- / n Worked example Mean wingspan of four dragonflies Individual wingspans follow N(4,.25). Take an SRS of four. Since the population is normal, x̄ is exactly normal. μx̄=4; σx̄=.25/√4=.125. For x̄>4.2, z=(4.2−4)/.125=1.60. P(x̄>4.2)=1−.9452=.0548. TI-84 normalcdf(4.2,1E99,4,.125). Excel =1-NORM.DIST(4.2,4,0.25/SQRT(4),TRUE) . Do not reuse σ. Using .25 instead of .125 would give z=.80 and the incorrect .2119. A mean of four has less variability than one observation. Worked example IQ for children with FAS The population has μ=70, σ=12. Take n=36. The course CLT guideline applies, since n≥30. σx̄=12/√36=2. For 64<x̄<71, z₁=−3.00 and z₂=.50. Table area=.6915−.0013=.6902; software=.6901. The middle 95% of means is 70±1.96(2)=(66.08,73.92). TI-84 normalcdf(64,71,70,2); invNorm(.025,70,2), invNorm(.975,70,2). Excel =NORM.DIST(71,70,2,TRUE)-NORM.DIST(64,70,2,TRUE) ."},{"t":"Center stays; spread shrinks","u":"module-6.html#summary","g":"Module 6 notes","x":"p̂ and x̄ are unbiased : their sampling distributions are centered at p and μ. Unbiased does not mean every sample estimate equals the parameter. Increasing n reduces spread. Multiplying n by four halves the SD; multiplying n by nine divides it by three. Do not confuse the size of each sample with the number of simulated samples: the former changes the theoretical SD, while the latter improves how clearly a simulation reveals it. Based on Module 6 lecture notes. Conditions and scale distinctions are made explicit for hand calculations. Quadruple the sample, halve the spread. Because x = / n , multiplying n by 4 divides the standard deviation by 4 = 2 . The same is true for p ."},{"t":"Estimate a fixed population parameter","u":"module-7.html#estimation","g":"Module 7 notes","x":"A point estimate is a single best guess; a confidence interval adds a margin to describe precision. For a proportion, estimate p with p̂; for a mean, estimate μ with x̄. A standard error (SE) estimates the SD of a statistic when population variability is unknown. Construct intervals for p and μ, checking their conditions. Find critical values from the normal and t tables. Interpret confidence correctly and recover a margin from endpoints. Plan n for a desired margin of error. confidence interval=point estimate of error The confidence level C describes the long-run capture rate of the method . Its error rate is α=1−C. The realized interval and the population parameter are fixed once the data are collected; we do not assign a 95% probability to a fixed parameter falling in that one interval. Not sure which method a problem needs? Use the method guide : three questions point to the right interval, test, or sample-size formula, and the drill gives you practice recognizing each one."},{"t":"Confidence interval for p","u":"module-7.html#proportion-ci","g":"Module 7 notes","x":"SE p= p(1- p)n, p z*SE p Conditions: random data, approximate independence, at least 10 observed successes and 10 observed failures . Use p̂ in the interval SE because p is unknown. Critical values: 80% → 1.282; 90% → 1.645; 95% → 1.960; 99% → 2.576. Each tail has area (1−C)/2. Worked example Does the sports drink improve energy? An SRS of 150 volunteers includes 72 who report improvement. Construct a 95% CI. Parameter: true proportion of people who notice improved energy. p̂=72/150=.48. There are 72 successes and 78 failures, so both counts pass. SE=√[.48(.52)/150]=.04079… z*=1.960; margin=1.960(.04079…)=.07995… CI=.48±.07995…=(.4000,.5600), rounded to (.40,.56). We are 95% confident the true proportion who notice improved energy lies between .40 and .56. TI-84 STAT → TESTS → 1-PropZInt. x=72, n=150, C-Level=.95 → Calculate. Excel =72/150-1.96*SQRT((72/150)*(1-72/150)/150) . Replace minus by plus for the upper bound. The manufacturer’s target .35 is below this interval. Within this analysis, all plausible values exceed .35. Design and representativeness still matter when deciding where the result generalizes. Use the right counts. For a proportion CI, check successes and failures in the observed sample. For a proportion test in Module 8, use expected counts under p₀ instead. Worked example 90% CI In an SRS of 1820 people, 364 agreed with a statement. p = 0.2 ; 364 successes and 1456 failures. 0.2 1.645 0.2(0.8)/1820 = 0.2 0.015 = (0.185,\\ 0.215) . Common mistakes. Using z* for the wrong confidence level. Leaving n out of the square root. Rounding p or the SE too early; store them instead."},{"t":"Why t replaces z for a mean","u":"module-7.html#t-table","g":"Module 7 notes","x":"Population σ is usually unknown, so estimate it with sample s. This extra uncertainty leads to the t distribution for the standardized sample mean under normal sampling. The sample mean itself is not “t-distributed”; the standardized statistic is. t is centered at zero, has heavier tails than normal, and approaches normal as degrees of freedom increase. SE x= s n, T= x- s/ n, df=n-1 To read t*: calculate df, choose the next smaller listed df if needed, and use upper-tail area (1−C)/2. The t table has df 1–30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 1000. Software uses actual df and may give a slightly smaller critical value. Worked example A 99% interval for a mean facial ratio n=40, x̄=1.524, s=.106. The data show no extreme skew or extreme outliers. SE=.106/√40=.01676…; actual df=39. For the hand table, use df=30. Upper-tail area=.005 gives t*=2.750. Margin=2.750(.01676…)=.04609… Table CI=1.524±.04609…=(1.478,1.570), rounded. The lecture’s software calculation uses actual df=39, t*=2.708, giving (1.479,1.569). Both are shown so you can distinguish the conservative table rule from the exact check. TI-84 TInterval → Stats; x̄=1.524, Sx=.106, n=40, C-Level=.99. For table-row t*: invT(.995,30). Excel Exact upper bound: =1.524+T.INV(0.995,39)*0.106/SQRT(40) . Use df 30 for the table check."},{"t":"Confidence interval for μ","u":"module-7.html#mean-ci","g":"Module 7 notes","x":"x t* s n Conditions: randomly gathered, approximately independent data, and no extreme skew or extreme outliers . Small departures from normality can be acceptable; extreme observations are a concern because x̄ and s are not resistant. Inspect the distribution rather than declaring every large sample automatically safe. Worked example Mean percentage of dietary fat n=165, x̄=35.294, s=6.981. Find a 95% CI. SE=6.981/√165=.54346… Actual df=164; the hand table uses df=100, t*=1.984. Table margin≈1.078; CI≈(34.216,36.372). Exact software t*=1.9745… gives the lecture interval (34.221,36.367). State the conclusion about the population mean dietary fat percentage, not individual nurses’ diets. TI-84 STAT → TESTS → TInterval → Stats. Enter x̄, Sx, n, and .95. Excel =35.294-T.INV(0.975,164)*6.981/SQRT(165) ; use plus for the upper bound."},{"t":"Margin, width, and interpretation","u":"module-7.html#margin","g":"Module 7 notes","x":"estimate= L+U2, m= U-L2, =2m For (.40,.56), the center is .48, width .16, and margin .08. Higher confidence requires a wider interval. Larger n narrows it, with SE proportional to 1/√n. More variable observations make it wider. Do not reduce confidence after looking at results just to produce a desired conclusion. TI-84 Center: (.40+.56)/2. Margin: (.56−.40)/2. Excel =AVERAGE(0.4,0.56) ; =(0.56-0.4)/2 . “95% confident” means that about 95% of intervals constructed by this procedure over repeated random samples capture the fixed parameter. It does not mean 95% of individual observations lie in the interval or 95% of samples have the same estimate. Wrong interpretations to avoid. \"There is a 95% probability that p is in (0.40, 0.56).\" \"95% of the data fall in the interval.\" \"95% of sample proportions fall in this interval.\" The interval is about the parameter, and the 95% is about the method."},{"t":"Plan precision before collecting data","u":"module-7.html#sample-size","g":"Module 7 notes","x":"np= (z*)2 p(1- p)m2 , n 4s2m2 \\;at 95\\% For p, use a pilot p̂ or the conservative .5 if no estimate is available. Use m as a proportion (.05 for five percentage points). For μ, the course’s 95% planning approximation uses t*≈2; obtain s from a pilot study if necessary. Always round n up. Worked example Smaller margins need more observations Verve: p̂=.48, 95% confidence, m=.05. n≥1.96²(.48)(.52)/.05²=383.531… → 384. Nurses: s=6.981, 95% confidence, m=.5. n≥4(6.981)²/.5²=779.75… → 780. Rounding down would fail the precision target. The second formula is a planning approximation, not an exact small-sample guarantee. TI-84 Calculate each expression, inspect its fractional part, and round up to the next integer. Excel =ROUNDUP(1.96^2*0.48*0.52/0.05^2,0) ; =ROUNDUP(4*6.981^2/0.5^2,0) . Based on Module 7 lecture notes. Exact lecture intervals are distinguished from the required conservative table method."},{"t":"A claim, then evidence","u":"module-8.html#logic","g":"Module 8 notes","x":"Earlier modules estimated a parameter with a point estimate and a confidence interval. Now we use probability to test a claim about a parameter such as a proportion p or a mean μ. A hypothesis is a claim that a population parameter takes a particular value or falls in a range of values. Null hypothesis H₀: the statement of no effect, assumed true until shown to be false. We look for evidence against it. Alternative hypothesis Hₐ: the statement we hope to demonstrate. We look for evidence for it. State both hypotheses (and α) before gathering or analyzing data. Choosing them after seeing the data is unethical. H0:p=p0\\ or \\ H0: = 0; Ha:\\ >,\\ <,\\ or\\ We cannot prove either hypothesis without a census; we can only weigh the evidence. The test is a proof by contradiction : assume H₀ is true, collect data, and ask whether the data contradict that assumption. It works like a court case: the defendant is presumed innocent (H₀) unless there is evidence of guilt (Hₐ) beyond a reasonable doubt. If the data are consistent with H₀, or give little support to Hₐ, we fail to reject H₀. If the data strongly support Hₐ, we reject H₀. Claims with “at least” or “at most”. Management claims mean income is at least $500. That is the status quo, so it goes in H0: = 500 , and the suspicion goes in Ha: < 500 . In this course H0 is always written with “=”. Use the parameters p and μ, never the statistics p̂ and x̄. Worked example Therapeutic touch: setting up the test Ten blindfolded therapeutic-touch (TT) practitioners each had a researcher hold her hand over one of their hands, chosen at random, 10 times. They identified the correct hand 52 times out of 100. Parameter: p = the true proportion of times a TT practitioner can perceive the energy field (picks the correct hand). If they are just guessing, they will be right about 50% of the time. H0: p = 0.5 (at most half the time, just guessing); Ha: p > 0.5 (more than half the time). The data lean toward Hₐ because p = 52/100 = 0.52 > 0.5 . But is 0.52 far enough above 0.5? We need to measure the strength of the evidence: if H₀ were true, how unusual would a sample like this be? Test statistics A test statistic (TS) counts how many standard errors the estimate is from the hypothesized value of the parameter. TS= estimate-hypothesized value of parameterstandard error of estimate A TS close to 0 means the data are compatible with H₀. An extreme TS (far from 0) means the data are not compatible with H₀. If the TS is extreme, either H₀ is true and we drew a very unusual sample (possible but unlikely), or H₀ is false (more plausible, and what statisticians choose to believe)."},{"t":"Critical values and the rejection region","u":"module-8.html#critical","g":"Module 8 notes","x":"We need an objective rule for deciding when a test statistic is extreme enough to reject H₀. The rejection region is the set of test-statistic values so extreme they would be unlikely if H₀ were true. Its boundary is the critical value , z* or t* , read from the t-table. The area of the rejection region equals the significance level α . z* −z* −z* z* Hₐ: > reject if TS ≥ z* Hₐ: < reject if TS ≤ −z* Hₐ: ≠ reject if |TS| ≥ z* Shaded: the rejection region, total area α. For a two-sided test each tail has area α/2. How to find it. First assume H₀ is true: this gives the distribution of the test statistic (Z for a proportion, tn-1 for a mean). Then find the critical value that cuts off area α in the direction of Hₐ. TS in the rejection region: the data are too unusual to believe H₀, so reject H₀ . We have not proven anything, but there is statistically significant evidence at the α level for Hₐ. TS not in the rejection region: the data are consistent with H₀, so fail to reject H₀ . There is not enough evidence at the α level for Hₐ. The significance level α Decide in advance how much evidence against H₀ you require. The common levels are 0.10, 0.05 and 0.01, with α = 0.05 the default . Setting α = 0.05 means we require evidence so strong it would happen less than 5% of the time if H₀ were true. Finding critical values with the t-table Columns give the upper-tail probability. One-sided test: use column α. Two-sided test: use column α/2. Rows give the degrees of freedom. Mean: row df=n-1 , critical value t* . Proportion: the bottom z* row. If your df is not listed, use the next smaller df in the table. Sign: Hₐ with >: reject if TS critical value . Hₐ with <: reject if TS -critical value . Hₐ with : reject if |TS| critical value . Common z* values (bottom row of the t-table) α One-sided Two-sided 0.10 1.282 1.645 0.05 1.645 1.960 0.01 2.326 2.576 Worked example Reading critical values Proportion, Hₐ: p > p₀, α = 0.01: z* row, column 0.01, so z*=2.326 . Reject if TS ≥ 2.326. Proportion, Hₐ: p ≠ p₀, α = 0.05: z* row, column α/2 = 0.025, so z*=1.960 . Reject if |TS| ≥ 1.960. Mean, n = 8, Hₐ: μ < μ₀, α = 0.05: row df = 7, column 0.05, so t*=1.895 . Reject if TS ≤ −1.895. Mean, n = 165, Hₐ: μ ≠ μ₀, α = 0.01: df = 164 is not in the table, so use the next smaller row, df = 100; column 0.005 gives t*=2.626 . Reject if |TS| ≥ 2.626. TI-84 check z*: 2nd VARS → invNorm(1−α) for one-sided, invNorm(1−α/2) for two-sided. t*: invT(1−α, df). The calculator uses the exact df (invT(.995,164) = 2.605), so it can differ slightly from the table’s next-smaller-row value. Excel check =NORM.S.INV(1-0.01) gives 2.326; =T.INV(1-0.05,7) gives 1.895. Two ways to decide. The rejection region is one way to make the decision. The next section shows the other, the p-value approach . Both always give the same decision, so on a quiz or exam you may use either one."},{"t":"The p-value approach","u":"module-8.html#p-value","g":"Module 8 notes","x":"Instead of asking whether the test statistic lands in the rejection region, ask: if H₀ were true, how surprising is a test statistic this extreme? The p-value is the probability, assuming H₀ is true, of getting a test statistic at least as extreme as the one observed, in the direction of Hₐ. A small p-value means data like ours would be rare if H₀ were true: strong evidence against H₀. A large p-value means data like ours are common under H₀: little evidence against it. The p-value follows the direction of Hₐ (use Z for a proportion, T with df = n − 1 for a mean) Alternative p-value Area Ha: p>p0 or > 0 P(Z TS) to the right of TS Ha: p<p0 or < 0 P(Z TS) to the left of TS Ha: p p0 or 0 2P(Z |TS|) both tails beyond |TS| Reject H0 if p-value ; otherwise fail to reject H0. The conclusion is written exactly as before: there [is / is not] sufficient evidence at the α level that the parameter is [greater than / less than / different from] the null value. Finding the p-value for a proportion (normal table) Round z to two decimals and read the left area from the standard normal table. Hₐ with >: p-value = 1 − (left area at z). Hₐ with <: p-value = left area at z. Hₐ with ≠: p-value = 2 × (1 − left area at |z|). Do not forget to double. Beyond ±3.49 the table runs out: the tail area is essentially 0, so the p-value is less than 0.0001. Finding the p-value for a mean (t-table gives an interval) The t-table lists only a few tail areas, so by hand you can only say which two areas the p-value falls between. Go to row df=n-1 (the next smaller df if yours is not listed). Find the two table values that |TS| falls between. The column headings above them are the two tail areas. One-sided test: the p-value is between those two areas. Two-sided test: double both. If |TS| is beyond the last column, the p-value is less than 0.0005 (less than 0.001 for a two-sided test). If it is below the first column, the p-value is more than 0.25 (more than 0.5 two-sided). That interval is enough to decide whenever α is not inside it. The exact p-value comes from software: on the TI, tcdf ; in Excel, T.DIST . Worked example Reading p-values from the tables Proportion, Hₐ: p > 0.5, z = 0.40. The left area at 0.40 is 0.6554, so p-value = 1 − 0.6554 = 0.3446. Proportion, Hₐ: p ≠ 0.3, z = −2.10. The left area at −2.10 is 0.0179, so p-value = 2(0.0179) = 0.0358. Mean, Hₐ: μ < 5, t = −3.204, n = 8. Row df = 7: 3.204 is between 2.998 (column 0.01) and 3.499 (column 0.005), so 0.005 < p-value < 0.01. Exactly, p-value = 0.0075. Mean, Hₐ: μ ≠ 30, t = 2.20, n = 20. Row df = 19: 2.20 is between 2.093 (column 0.025) and 2.205 (column 0.02). Two-sided, so double: 0.04 < p-value < 0.05. At α = 0.05, the p-value is below α, so reject H₀. TI-84 check 2ND → DISTR. normalcdf(0.4,1E99) = .3446; tcdf(-1E99,-3.204,7) = .0075. For a two-sided test, double the one-tail area. Excel check =1-NORM.S.DIST(0.4,TRUE) gives 0.3446; =T.DIST(-3.204,7,TRUE) gives 0.0075; =T.DIST.2T(2.2,19) gives 0.0404. Why the two methods always agree The rejection region is the tail with area α. The test statistic lands in it exactly when the area beyond the test statistic, the p-value, is at most α. In every worked example below, use the Decide with switch on the graph to watch both methods reach the same decision. Common mistakes. Forgetting to double the tail area for a two-sided test. Reading the left area when Hₐ has > (subtract from 1). Comparing the p-value with α/2 instead of α (the doubling already accounts for both tails). Saying the p-value is the probability that H₀ is true: it is the probability of data this extreme if H₀ is true. Not sure which method a problem needs? Use the method guide : three questions point to the right interval, test, or sample-size formula, and the drill gives you practice recognizing each one."},{"t":"Test a single proportion","u":"module-8.html#proportion-test","g":"Module 8 notes","x":"Hypotheses. H0:p=p0 versus one of Ha:p>p0 , Ha:p<p0 (one-sided) or Ha:p p0 (two-sided), where p is the unknown population proportion and p₀ its hypothesized value. Assumptions. Normality: both np0 10 and n(1-p0) 10 (the expected numbers of successes and failures if H₀ is true). Independence: randomly gathered data. TS=z= p-p0 p0(1-p0)n N(0,1) Rejection region. If Hₐ: p > p₀, reject H₀ if TS ≥ z*. If Hₐ: p < p₀, reject if TS ≤ −z*. If Hₐ: p ≠ p₀, reject if |TS| ≥ z*. The critical value z* comes from the bottom row of the t-table, column α (one-sided) or α/2 (two-sided). Decision and conclusion. If the TS falls in the rejection region, reject H₀; otherwise fail to reject (FTR) H₀. Then write: “There is / is not sufficient evidence at the α level that the proportion of [variable of interest] is [greater than / less than / different from] p₀.” Always state the conclusion in terms of Hₐ. Worked example Can TT practitioners perceive an energy field? =0.01 Hypotheses: H0:p=0.5 , Ha:p>0.5 . Assumptions: np0=n(1-p0)=100(0.5)=50 10 ; the hand was chosen at random each time. Test statistic: z= 0.52-0.5 0.5(0.5)/100= 0.020.05=0.4 . Critical value: upper-tailed, α = 0.01, z* row, column 0.01, so z*=2.326 . Reject H₀ if TS ≥ 2.326. Decision: TS = 0.4 < 2.326 is not in the rejection region, so we fail to reject H₀. Or with the p-value: P(Z 0.40)=1-0.6554=0.3446 , and 0.3446 > α = 0.01, so we fail to reject H₀. Same decision. Conclusion: there is insufficient evidence at the α = 0.01 level that the true proportion of times a TT practitioner can perceive a human energy field is greater than 0.5. TI-84 check STAT → TESTS → 5:1-PropZTest: p₀ = .5, x = 52, n = 100, prop > p₀. It reports z = .4 (and a p-value of .3446 > .01, the same decision). Excel check =(52/100-0.5)/SQRT(0.5*0.5/100) gives 0.4; =NORM.S.INV(0.99) gives 2.326. Worked example Can humans really randomize? =0.05 A random sample of 189 people chose a “random” number from 1 to 10. If choices were random, each number would be picked 10% of the time, but 69 chose 7. Is the probability of choosing 7 different from 10%? Hypotheses: H0:p=0.1 , Ha:p 0.1 . Assumptions: np0=189(0.1)=18.9 10 and n(1-p0)=189(0.9)=170.1 10 ; a random sample. Test statistic: z= 69/189-0.1 0.1(0.9)/189=12.15 . Critical value: two-sided, α = 0.05, z* row, column α/2 = 0.025, so z*=1.960 . Reject H₀ if |TS| ≥ 1.960. Decision: TS = 12.15 ≥ 1.960 is in the rejection region, so we reject H₀. Or with the p-value: 2P(Z 12.15) is far beyond the table, so the p-value is less than 0.0001, well below α = 0.05: reject H₀. Same decision. Conclusion: there is sufficient evidence at the 0.05 level that the probability a person chooses 7 from the numbers 1 through 10 is not 0.1. Worked example Are pro-choice adults in the minority? =0.05 A Gallup poll of 1,014 randomly selected adults found 48% consider themselves pro-choice. One group used this as evidence that pro-choice adults are a minority. Hypotheses: H0:p=0.50 , Ha:p<0.50 . Assumptions: np0=n(1-p0)=1014(0.5)=507 10 ; a random sample. Test statistic: z= 0.48-0.50 0.5(0.5)/1014=-1.2737 . Critical value: lower-tailed, α = 0.05, z* row, column 0.05, so z*=1.645 . Reject H₀ if TS ≤ −1.645. Decision: TS = −1.2737 > −1.645 is not in the rejection region, so we fail to reject H₀. Or with the p-value: round to z = −1.27; P(Z -1.27)=0.1020 , and 0.1020 > α = 0.05, so we fail to reject H₀. Same decision. Conclusion: there is insufficient evidence at the 0.05 level that the proportion of adults who are pro-choice is less than 0.5. A sample proportion below 0.5 is not, by itself, enough. Common mistakes. Using p̂ in the standard error (a test assumes H₀, so it uses p₀; only a confidence interval uses p̂). Using column α instead of α/2 for a two-sided test. Forgetting the minus sign for a lower-tailed test: reject when TS ≤ −z*. Saying “accept H₀”: we only fail to reject it."},{"t":"Test a single mean with t","u":"module-8.html#mean-test","g":"Module 8 notes","x":"So far every example used categorical data, a sample proportion p̂ and the Z distribution. For quantitative data we use the sample mean x̄ and the tn-1 distribution to make inference about the population mean μ. Hypotheses. H0: = 0 versus Ha: > 0 , Ha: < 0 or Ha: 0 . Assumptions. Normality: no extreme skew or extreme outliers (check a histogram, boxplot or normal quantile plot; t procedures depend on x̄ and s, which are strongly affected by them). Independence: randomly gathered data. TS=t= x- 0s/ n TS tn-1 Rejection region. If Hₐ: μ > μ₀, reject H₀ if TS ≥ t*. If Hₐ: μ < μ₀, reject if TS ≤ −t*. If Hₐ: μ ≠ μ₀, reject if |TS| ≥ t*. Find t* in the t-table, row df=n-1 (next smaller if not listed), column α (one-sided) or α/2 (two-sided). Decide and conclude as for a proportion: “There is / is not sufficient evidence at the α level that the mean of [variable] is [greater than / less than / different from] μ₀.” Worked example Is there enough oxygen for the fish? =0.05 Fish are dying in a river. Fish need a mean oxygen level of at least 5.0 ppm. A biologist sampled 8 randomly chosen days: x=4.8133 , s=0.1648 ppm, and the plots show no extreme skew or outliers. Hypotheses: H0: =5 , Ha: <5 . Assumptions: no extreme skew or outliers; random days. Test statistic: t= 4.8133-50.1648/ 8=-3.204 with df=8-1=7 . Critical value: lower-tailed, α = 0.05, row df = 7, column 0.05, so t*=1.895 . Reject H₀ if TS ≤ −1.895. Decision: TS = −3.204 ≤ −1.895 is in the rejection region, so we reject H₀. Or with the p-value: P(T -3.204) with df = 7. In row 7, 3.204 is between 2.998 (column 0.01) and 3.499 (column 0.005), so 0.005 < p-value < 0.01; exactly, p-value = 0.0075. Either way it is below α = 0.05: reject H₀. Same decision. Conclusion: there is sufficient evidence at the 0.05 level that the mean oxygen content is less than 5.0 ppm. TI-84 check STAT → TESTS → 2:T-Test → Stats: μ₀ = 5, x̄ = 4.8133, Sx = .1648, n = 8, μ < μ₀. It reports t = −3.204. invT(.05,7) = −1.895. Excel check =(4.8133-5)/(0.1648/SQRT(8)) gives −3.204; =T.INV(0.05,7) gives −1.895. Worked example Nurses’ calories from fat =0.01 An SRS of 165 female nurses gave x=35.294\\% of calories from fat with s=6.981 , and no extreme skew or outliers. Is the mean percentage different from 30? Hypotheses: H0: =30 , Ha: 30 . Test statistic: t= 35.294-306.981/ 165=9.741 with df=164 . Critical value: two-sided, α = 0.01, column α/2 = 0.005. df = 164 is not in the table, so use the next smaller row, df = 100: t*=2.626 . Reject H₀ if |TS| ≥ 2.626. Decision: TS = 9.741 ≥ 2.626 is in the rejection region, so we reject H₀. Or with the p-value: in row df = 100, 9.741 is beyond the last column (0.0005), so the two-sided p-value is less than 2(0.0005) = 0.001, below α = 0.01: reject H₀. Same decision. Conclusion: there is sufficient evidence at the 0.01 level that the average percentage of calories from fat is different from 30. Worked example Is entry-level pay at least $500 a week? =0.05 Management claims the mean weekly income of entry-level assembly-line workers is at least $500. A random sample of 9 workers gives x=\\$441.11 and s=\\$12.69 , with no extreme skew or outliers. Hypotheses: H0: =500 , Ha: <500 . Test statistic: t= 441.11-50012.69/ 9=-13.922 with df=8 . Critical value: lower-tailed, α = 0.05, row df = 8, column 0.05, so t*=1.860 . Reject H₀ if TS ≤ −1.860. Decision: TS = −13.922 ≤ −1.860 is in the rejection region, so we reject H₀. Or with the p-value: in row df = 8, 13.922 is beyond the last column, so the p-value is less than 0.0005, below α = 0.05: reject H₀. Same decision. Conclusion: there is sufficient evidence at the 0.05 level that the true mean income of entry-level assembly-line workers is less than $500 per week, contradicting management’s claim. Summary Every test follows the same steps Hypotheses , stated before gathering data; the new theory or claim goes in Hₐ. Assumptions: normality and independence. Test statistic: (estimate − hypothesized value) / standard error. Decide, ei"},{"t":"Errors, α, β, and power","u":"module-8.html#errors-power","g":"Module 8 notes","x":"Decision H₀ is true H₀ is false Reject H₀ Type I error Correct decision Fail to reject H₀ Correct decision Type II error Type I error: rejecting H₀ when H₀ is true. =P(Type I error) , the significance level. It is usually considered more serious, so we set α before running the test. Type II error: failing to reject H₀ when H₀ is false. =P(Type II error) . α and β are inversely related: lowering one raises the other. Increase n to lower both at once. Setting α = 0.05 means that when H₀ is true there is a 5% chance of drawing a sample whose TS lands in the rejection region. Worked example Which error could we have made? We test H0: =30 vs Ha: 30 and the TS falls in the rejection region, so we reject H₀. If we later learn that μ = 30, H₀ was true: that decision was a Type I error . Therapeutic touch: we failed to reject H₀ at α = 0.01. The possible error is Type II (failing to reject a false H₀). Consequence: people would not seek care that could actually help. Nurses’ fat intake: we rejected H₀ at α = 0.01. The possible error is Type I (rejecting a true H₀). Consequence: we would wrongly conclude the nurses’ average fat intake differs from 30% when it does not. Planning a study: power When choosing a sample size, consider the significance level (how much protection against a Type I error), the effect size (how large a difference matters in practice: |pa-p0| for a proportion, | a- 0| for a mean), and the power (how sure we want to be of detecting that effect). Power=P(reject H0 H0 is false)=1- The power against a specific alternative is the probability the test rejects H₀ at the chosen α when that alternative is true. It is the probability of a correct decision, so higher is better. If a test has power 0.98 and H₀ is false, 98% of all samples lead us to reject H₀. Software calculates power, so we focus on interpreting it. Worked example Does diet cola lose sweetness? Tasters score a cola’s sweetness from 1 to 10, then again after four months of storage. The differences (before − after) show whether sweetness is lost. Hypotheses: H0: =0 vs Ha: >0 , where μ is the mean loss in sweetness score. Significance level: the manufacturer accepts a Type I error up to 5% of the time (α = 0.05). Effect size: a mean loss of 0.8 points will be noticed by consumers. Power: they want to be 90% sure the test detects a mean loss of 0.8, so probability at least 0.9 that a test at α = 0.05 rejects H₀ when a=0.8 . Software then gives the number of tasters needed. =1-Power=1-0.9=0.1 , because when H₀ is false the only two outcomes are rejecting (power) and failing to reject (β). Worked example Caffeine You plan to test whether college students consume more than 25 mg of caffeine per week on average, with an SRS of 64 students. If the true mean is 30 mg, the test has power 0.85. Hypotheses: H0: =25 vs Ha: >25 . Significance level α = 0.05 (the default); effect size | a- 0|=30-25=5 ; power = P(reject a false H₀) = 0.85. Interpretation: if the true mean is 30 mg, 85% of all possible samples of size 64 would lead us to reject H₀. =1-0.85=0.15 . Ways to increase power: increase α, decrease β, increase the sample size n (what we typically do), or increase the effect size. The picture: the H₀ distribution of the statistic is split at the critical value into “correct, 1 − α” and “Type I error, α”; the Hₐ distribution is split at the same line into “Type II error, β” and “correct, power = 1 − β”. The lab below lets you watch this happen."},{"t":"Significance, usefulness, and CIs","u":"module-8.html#uses-abuses","g":"Module 8 notes","x":"A test has statistical significance when it leads us to reject H₀ in favor of Hₐ, that is, when the test statistic falls in the rejection region. Every decision to reject is statistically significant. A rejection has practical significance only if the difference between the statistic and the hypothesized value, | p-p0| or | x- 0| , is large enough to matter: large enough to influence real decisions and meaningful to the people affected. Not every statistically significant result is practically significant. Worked example Is the faster headache pill worth it? Brand A costs $0.10 per pill and Brand B $1.50. A randomized experiment with 2,000 volunteers found mean time to relief 10.06 minutes for A and 9.95 minutes for B, and the test statistic fell in the rejection region at α = 0.05. Hypotheses: H0: A= B vs Ha: A B (μ = mean time to relief for each brand). Statistically significant? Yes: the TS fell in the rejection region at α = 0.05. Practically significant? Would you pay $1.40 more per pill for relief 0.11 minutes (6.6 seconds) sooner? No. Why was it significant? The sample was very large. Extremely large samples almost always give extreme test statistics and a decision to reject H₀. Confidence intervals and two-sided tests Both rely on the sampling distribution of x̄ or p̂. A two-sided test at level α can be carried out with a confidence interval at level C=1- : If the interval contains the hypothesized value, it is plausible: fail to reject H₀. If the interval does not contain it, it is not plausible: reject H₀. Worked example Same decision, two ways Nurses: testing H0: =30 vs Ha: 30 at α = 0.01 gave TS = 9.741 with df = 164. Reject, because |TS| = 9.741 ≥ t* = 2.626. The 99% confidence interval for μ is (33.877, 36.710). It does not contain 30, so 30 is not plausible and we reject H₀: the same decision. Testing H0:p=0.4 vs Ha:p 0.4 , a 90% CI for p is (0.28, 0.42). At α = 0.10 the interval contains 0.4, so 0.4 is plausible and we fail to reject H₀. The test statistic will therefore not fall in the rejection region: |TS| < z* = 1.645. Summary Uses and abuses Not every rejection is practically significant. Statistical significance: the TS falls in the rejection region. Practical significance: a difference large enough to matter. =P(Type I error)=P(reject H0 H0 true) ; =P(Type II error)=P(FTR H0 H0 false) . They are inversely related; increase n to lower both. Power=1- =P(reject H0 H0 false) . To increase power: increase α, n or the effect size; decrease β. A confidence interval is equivalent to a two-sided test: hypothesized value inside the interval, fail to reject; outside, reject. Read the strength of the claim. Failing to reject does not show H₀ is true; the test may simply lack power. Do not choose hypotheses or α after seeing the data, keep testing until something lands in the rejection region, or report only favorable results. Based on Module 8 lecture notes."},{"t":"Answer three questions","u":"choose.html#chooser-h","g":"Method guide","x":"Answer three questions The guide needs JavaScript. The summary table below has the same information."},{"t":"six methods at a glance","u":"choose.html#summary-h","g":"Method guide","x":"The six methods at a glance Start with the variable . If each individual gives a category (yes/no, success/failure, prefers A), you are working with a proportion p . If each individual gives a measured number (minutes, pounds, a score), you are working with a mean μ . Then ask what the question wants: a range of plausible values (confidence interval), a verdict on a claim about one specific value (hypothesis test), or how many to sample (sample size). Method Formula Conditions Critical value p-value (tests) Words that point to it CI for p Module 7 p z* p(1- p)n Random; at least 10 successes and 10 failures in the sample z* row, column (1-C)/2 — “estimate the proportion”, “with 95% confidence”, “what percent of all…” Test for p Module 8 z= p-p0 p0(1-p0)/n Random; np0 10 and n(1-p0) 10 z* row, column (one-sided) or /2 (two-sided) Normal table: area beyond z toward Hₐ (double for ≠) “is there evidence that the proportion is more than / less than / different from …”, “claims 40% …” CI for μ Module 7 x t* s n Random; no extreme skew or outliers Row df=n-1 (next smaller if not listed), column (1-C)/2 — “estimate the mean”, “average … with 90% confidence” Test for μ Module 8 t= x- 0s/ n Random; no extreme skew or outliers Row df=n-1 , column or /2 t-table interval (row df = n − 1), or exact from tcdf / T.DIST “test the claim that the mean …”, “is the average higher than …” Sample size for p Module 7 n= ( z*m )2 p*(1-p*) , round up Use p*=0.5 with no prior estimate z* for the confidence level — “how many people should be surveyed to be within ±3 points” Sample size for μ Module 7 n= 4s2m2 , round up 95% confidence (course rule, t* 2 ) none — “how many observations to estimate the mean within ± …” Direction of Hₐ. “More than”, “increased”, “higher”, “exceeds” → > . “Less than”, “decreased”, “lower”, “fewer” → < . “Different from”, “changed”, “is not”, “differs” → . A claim of “at least” or “at most” is the status quo you are challenging: it becomes H0 (with =), and Hₐ points the other way. For example, “management claims pay is at least $500” gives Ha: <500 . Common mistakes. Using the proportion formulas for a measured variable (or the reverse). Writing a confidence interval when the question asks whether the data support a claim. Using p̂ in a test’s standard error (tests use p0 ). Using z* for a mean when only s is known (use t* ). Forgetting to double a two-sided p-value."},{"t":"Practice choosing","u":"choose.html#drill-h","g":"Method guide","x":"Practice choosing Each scenario is new. Pick the method (and, for a test, the direction of Hₐ) without computing anything. The goal is to recognize the setting quickly."},{"t":"standard normal table","u":"tables.html#t-table","g":"Tables & formulas","x":"The standard normal table The entries give left area P(Z<z) , rounded to four decimals. Round z to two decimals. Negative rows use a negative hundredths digit too: row −1.6, column .04 means −1.64. Look up z → left area Left area → z Value Find in table For z beyond ±3.49, the course treats left area as essentially 0 or 1. For inverse lookups, choose the closest four-decimal entry. Ties use the midpoint z, such as .9500 → 1.645."},{"t":"t table","u":"tables.html#formulas","g":"Tables & formulas","x":"The t table Columns show upper-tail area . For a confidence interval use (1−C)/2. For a test, use column α (one-sided) or α/2 (two-sided). For a p-value, find the two columns the test statistic falls between. For unlisted df, use the next smaller row. The bottom row gives z*, the critical value for a proportion. Which row and column? It depends on the method. The method guide walks you from the problem to the right interval or test, and tells you where its critical value is. Degrees of freedom Look up Confidence → t* Test: α → critical value Test: TS → p-value interval Confidence (%), α, or TS Alternative tail Two-sided (≠) Right (>) Left (<) Find in table Table values are rounded to three decimals. Software checks use actual df and full precision."},{"t":"formula sheet","u":"tables.html","g":"Tables & formulas","x":"The formula sheet Choose the method from the variable type and research question. Verify the conditions before substituting numbers. Sample descriptions x= xin, s= (xi- x)2n-1 Quartiles and fences IQR=Q3-Q1, Q1-1.5IQR,\\;Q3+1.5IQR Regression b1=R sysx,\\;b0= y-b1 x,\\;e=y- y Conditional probability P(B A)= P(A B)P(A) Normal and inverse normal z= x- , x= +z Binomial probability P(X=k)= nkpk(1-p)n-k Binomial center and spread =np, = np(1-p) Sampling distributions p= p(1-p)n, x= n CI for a proportion p z* p(1- p)n CI for a mean x t* s n, df=n-1 Plan n for p n= (z*)2 p(1- p)m2 Plan n for μ at 95% n 4s2m2 Proportion test z= p-p0 p0(1-p0)/n Mean test t= x- 0s/ n, df=n-1 p-value by the direction of Hₐ >:\\ P(Z TS),\\; <:\\ P(Z TS),\\; :\\ 2P(Z |TS|) Confidence critical values 80\\%:1.282,\\;90\\%:1.645,\\;95\\%:1.960,\\;99\\%:2.576 Power and decision power=1- , p H0 Course conventions. Quartiles exclude the median from both halves when n is odd. Use Sx for sample SD, σx for a probability distribution. Use no continuity correction in the normal approximation to the binomial. Proportion CI counts use p̂; test counts and SE use p₀. Always round a planned n up."}];
